[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mDesign — Section 2: error handling, testing, tooling[0m

  [1mCorrection to Section 1 first[0m, because it affects a filename you'd be living with. I said [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m. If we ever want
  to import that file in Node (for unit tests), plain [38;5;153m.js[39m is a problem: Node reads [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m to decide module
  format, there's no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so [38;5;153m.js[39m means CommonJS and an ESM import fails. Setting [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m would fix it
  [3mand break[0m [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, which are CommonJS and have nothing to do with this work — I'm not touching
  unrelated files to make my feature convenient. So if we want Node-testable modules, the new files are [1m[38;5;153mtracking.mjs[0m,
  which Node always treats as ESM and which browsers load fine. The one caveat: [38;5;153m.mjs[39m must be served as JavaScript, which
  current static servers do but a few older ones don't. Browsers never consult [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, so the browser path works
  either way. This choice only matters if you want unit tests — hence the question at the bottom.

  [1mError handling — the governing rule: telemetry must never break login.[0m [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m never throws. Three failure modes,
  each handled:

  - [1mStorage unavailable or throwing.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws in Safari private mode, when site data is blocked, and on quota

